        "bullets": [
            "Shipping through the Strait of Hormuz slowed following attacks on oil tankers, per two independent outlets citing data.",
            "Crisis Pressure holds in the extreme band while the Escalation Forecast has risen to elevated, the two indices diverging on direction.",
            "PortWatch recorded 1 transit on 2026-08-09, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; port arrivals across Hormuz-area ports reached 775 in the most recent 24 hours."
        ],
        "date": "2026-08-17",
        "generatedAt": "2026-08-17T08:53:26.373Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz is seeing materially reduced shipping activity following attacks on oil tankers, a development carried independently by two outlets and corroborated by the site's own traffic data. PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-08-09, recorded 1 transit against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that figure is now eight days old given PortWatch's weekly Tuesday publication cycle. Separately, port arrivals across five Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 775 in the most recent 24 hours, a distinct methodology that cannot be equated with the PortWatch transit count. At the time of writing, 406 vessels were holding position away from berth, a figure that excludes ships within 25 km of a working port. Brent was at $88.91 at 08:53 UTC, down 0.08% over 24 hours. The site's Hormuz Index composite shows Crisis Pressure in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast sits in the elevated band and is rising, driven primarily by Polymarket escalation contracts; that divergence between a flat state reading and a rising forecast reading is the principal analytical signal this morning. Iranian state media claim shipping through the strait slowed to a near halt over the weekend, a characterization not independently confirmed. U.S.-Iran negotiations remain unresolved, with no confirmed deal on strait access between those two parties.",
